<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Federation of Malaya</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1963 CE · capital Kuala Lumpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federation of Malay states and settlements that fought the communist Emergency and won independence from Britain in 1957.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/malayan-union">Malayan Union</a> on 1 February 1948, restoring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the nine Malay rulers and joining their states with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-of-wales-island">Penang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> under a British high commissioner. Months later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-communist-party">Malayan Communist Party</a> launched an armed revolt, and the government declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/briggs-plan">Briggs Plan</a> and High Commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-templer">Gerald Templer</a>, half a million rural Chinese were moved into new villages to cut off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>. The Alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">UMNO</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaysian-chinese-association">Malayan Chinese Association</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaysian-indian-congress">Malayan Indian Congress</a> won the 1955 election under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a>, who negotiated independence on 31 August 1957. A rotating king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-di-pertuan-agong">Yang di-Pertuan Agong</a>, was chosen from the rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Emergency was declared over in 1960, though communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> remained on the Thai border. On 16 September 1963 the federation joined with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Sarawak</a> to form <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, which kept its constitution and institutions.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federation-of-malaya.jpg" alt="Federation of Malaya" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Nightstallion assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malaya_(1950%E2%80%931963).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1963 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuala Lumpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gerald Templer, Tunku Abdul Rahman</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Malaysia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malaya, Persekutuan Tanah Melayu</dd></dl></aside></div>
